CONCLUSION (AC2): exactly one self-hosted job runs at a time across ALL 10 concurrently-triggered
workflow files; every other job — including jobs from workflow files DIFFERENT from the one currently
running — sits in a visible, API-queryable "pending" state (never "cancelled", never silently invisible)
until the runner frees up, then the next queued job starts. This is the queue:max/group behavior the AC
requires, observed live, not statically inferred.

CAVEAT / FINDING (see task_bugs in QA envelope): GitHub Actions' documented `concurrency:` schema supports
only `group` and `cancel-in-progress` — there is no `queue` key. `queue: max` is very likely a silently-
ignored/unsupported property (the workflow files still parsed and ran successfully with it present, so it
is not a hard validation error, but it is not a recognized concurrency mode either). The observed
serialize-and-wait (never cancel) behavior is fully explained by the job-level block's `cancel-in-progress`
being OMITTED, which defaults to `false` — i.e. the desired behavior works, but via the default, not via
"queue: max" doing anything. Flagged because a future maintainer could "fix" the apparently-dead `queue: max`
key by adding an explicit `cancel-in-progress: true`, which would silently flip the behavior to
cancel-on-collision and defeat the ticket's whole purpose.

Evidence channel used: GitHub REST + GraphQL API (api.github.com/repos/pokkeerr/nears/actions/*,
api.github.com/graphql) via the cached osxkeychain credential — the AC's sanctioned "UI/API" channel.
